498 13194139535576 17592186045443 true] [17592186518696 1083 #uuid "a8548bfa-8021-564f-bea6-e079f6532cca" 13194139535576 17592186045443 true] [17592186518697 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518707 13194139535576 17592186045443 true] [17592186518697 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535576 17592186045443 true] [17592186518697 1071 "The least common multiple of $5(y+9)^{2}$ and $3(y+9)^{2}$ is {{field-1}}. Use the positive integer LCM of the contents and a primitive polynomial part with positive leading coefficient." 13194139535576 17592186045443 true] [17592186518697 1072 #uuid "afa929ac-d9a8-57c4-a085-1e85b1b7ef81" 13194139535576 17592186045443 true] [17592186518697 1074 "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(5,3):$\n\n- Multiples of $5:$ $\\,5,\\,10,\\,15,\\,20,…$\n\n- Multiples of $3:$ $\\,3,\\,6,\\,9,\\,12,\\,15,\\,18,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(5,3)=15.$\n\nSince the variable part is the same in the expressions, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((y+9)^{2},\\,(y+9)^{2})=(y+9)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(5(y+9)^{2},\\,3(y+9)^{2})=15\\cdot (y+9)^{2}=15(y+9)^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535576 17592186045443 true] [17592186518698 1080 "2(3z^{2}+8z-7)" 13194139535576 17592186045443 true] [17592186518698 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535576 17592186045443 true] [17592186518698 1083 #uuid "b32b9d79-8d73-5553-add2-79b12f1ab3e3" 13194139535576 17592186045443 true] [17592186518699 1075 #edb/ref 17592186518668 13194139535576 17592186045443 true] [17592186518699 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139535576 17592186045443 true] [17592186518699 1077 "field-1" 13194139535576 17592186045443 true] [17592186518699 1078 #edb/ref 17592186518668 13194139535576 17592186045443 true] [17592186518699 1079